e-readable errors for invalid input, malformed strings, and buffer bounds."],[498,"Typed, machine-readable input, parse, and buffer errors."],[7,"typeid"],[159,"TypeID"],[207,"TypeID"],[285,"TypeID"],[661,"TypeID"],[665,"TypeID puts a lowercase prefix such as `user_` or `order_` in front of a base32-encoded UUID. It is useful when a public identifier should reveal the kind of resource it identifies. `typeid()` always generates a UUID v7, so new TypeIDs sort by creation time."],[6,"ulid"],[155,"ULID"],[281,"ULID"],[669,"ULID"],[671,"ULIDs sort by creation time and stay shorter than a hyphenated UUID. Use them when the value will often appear in URLs, logs, or APIs."],[393,"uniku"],[404,"uniku"],[263,"uniku / reference"],[411,"uniku also exposes `uuidv5.OID` and `uuidv5.X500`, and `uuidv5.namespace(ns)` to parse a namespace once for many names. See UUID v5."],[368,"uniku collects eleven ID generation strategies behind one consistent, type-safe API: UUID v4/v5/v7, ULID, TypeID, Nanoid, CUID v2, KSUID, ObjectID, XID, and TSID. Published benchmarks compare generation speed with dedicated npm alternatives for each format. Each strategy still has its own entry point, so your application ships only the code it imports."],[390,"uniku generates UUID v4, v5, and v7. There is no uniku equivalent for v1, v3, or v6; keep the `uuid` package for those, or generate them elsewhere and only use uniku for v4, v5, and v7 values."],[131,"uniku is ESM-only. Import the exact generator you want rather than a package-root barrel:"],[700,"uniku keeps no cache. If a hot loop derives the same names repeatedly, cache the results in your own code, with a size bound you choose."],[312,"uniku returns a `bigint`; the reference builds a TSID object"],[304,"uniku returns a string; the reference builds a KSUID object"],[288,"uniku returns a string; the reference builds an object, then calls `toString()`"],[424,"uniku v1 keeps its canonical identifier formats stable while finalizing its pre-v1 entry poi