500` (case-insensitive), or any UUID string (requires `-v 5`)"],[151,"UUID v7"],[277,"UUID v7"],[707,"UUID v7"],[709,"UUID v7 keeps the UUID shape while embedding a millisecond timestamp. It is a strong default for database primary keys when you want chronological locality without creating a custom ID column."],[204,"UUID v7 or ULID"],[208,"UUID v7 with a readable, domain-specific prefix."],[235,"UUID v7, ULID, KSUID, ObjectID, XID, and TSID embed a creation timestamp in the identifier itself. Anyone who can read the ID can decode when the record was created, not just its order relative to other records. That is a feature for index locality and a leak if the ID is public and the creation time is sensitive, for example a document, invoice, or account that should not reveal how old it is. If the identifier is exposed outside your system and creation time must stay private, use UUID v4, CUID v2, or Nanoid instead: none of them encode a timestamp."],[234,"UUID v7, ULID, ObjectID, XID, and TSID carry time information. uniku keeps the state required for monotonic sequencing inside one JavaScript isolate; it does not coordinate clocks or sequences between processes or machines."],[38,"UUID version: `4`, `5`, or `7` (default: `4`)"],[188,"UUIDs, ULIDs, TypeIDs, KSUIDs, ObjectIDs, XIDs, and TSIDs have a canonical binary representation. Their `toBytes()` and `fromBytes()` helpers let you store and transport that representation without inventing a second codec."],[431,"V1 code"],[15,"Validate"],[212,"Values that should resist enumeration"],[423,"What remains stable"],[264,"What the row actually compares"],[202,"Why"],[129,"Why byte helpers exist"],[684,"Why not memoize a random ID"],[481,"Within `1.x`, uniku will not remove or rename documented entry points, exports, options, methods, or constants. Canonical string formats, byte order, timestamp units, and documented error codes are part of the contract."],[663,"Wrapping an existing UUID"],[130,"Write into a caller-owned buffer"],[12,"xid"],[179,"XID"],[219,"XID"],[313,"XID"],[710,"XID"],[712,"XID is a 12-byte, lowercase base32hex identifier: a seconds-precision timestamp, a 40-bit per-runtime identity, and a shared 24-bit counter. Use it when you need Go's rs/xid wire format in a synchronous JavaScript runtime."],[135,"You do not import `toBytes()` or `timestamp()` separately. They stay attached to `uuidv7` and grouped with that strategy."]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":12,"2":13,"3":14,"4":15,"5":16,"6":17,"7":18,"8":19,"9":20,"10":21,"11":22,"12":23,"13":24,"14":25,"15":26,"16":27,"17":28,"18":29,"19":30,"20":31,"21":32,"22":33,"23":34,"24":35,"25":36,"26":37,"27":38,"28":39,"29":40,"30":41,"31":42,"32":43,"33":44,"34":45,"35":46,"36":47,"37":48,"38":49,"39":50,"40":51,"41":52,"42":53,"43":54,"44":55,"45":56,"46":57,"47":58,"48":59,"49":60,"50":61,"51":62,"52":63,"53":64,"54":65,"55":66,"56":67,"57":68,"58":69,"59":70,"60":71,"61":72,"62":73,"63":74,"64":75,"65":76,"66":77,"67":78,"68":79,"69":80,"70":81,"71":82,"72":83,"73":84,"74":85,"75":86,"76":87,"77":88,"78":89,"79":90,"80":91,"81":92,"82":93,"83":94,"84":95,"85":96,"86":97,"87":98,"88":99,"89":100,"90":101,"91":102,"92":103,"93":104,"94":105,"95":106,"96":107,"97":108,"98":109,"99":110,"100":111,"101":112,"102":113,"103":114,"104":115,"105":116,"106":117,"107":118,"108":119,"109":120,"110":121,"111":122,"112":123,"113":124,"114":125,"115":126,"116":127,"117":128,"118":129,"119":130,"120":131,"121":132,"122":133,"123":134,"124":135,"125":136,"126":137,"127":138,"128":139,"129":140,"130":141,"131":142,"132":143,"133":144,"134":145,"135":146,"136":147,"137":148,"138":149,"139":150,"140":151,"141":152,"142":153,"143":154,"144":155,"145":156,"146":157,"147":158,"148":159,"149":160,"150":161,"151":162,"152":163,"153":164,"154":165,"155":166,"156":167,"157":168,"158":169,"159":170,"160":171,"161":172,"162":173,"163":174,"164":175,"165":176,"166":177,"167":178,"168":179,"169":180,"170":181,"171":182,"172":183,"173":184,"174"